ng—males and females. The comparison is with respect to the response variable score. Here is a figure that summarizes the example:","position":{"start":{"line":50,"column":1},"end":{"line":50,"column":1}},"key":"SLpsPZzfwP"}],"key":"oa7ntmf8Ow"},{"type":"container","kind":"figure","children":[{"type":"image","url":"/textbook/build/m16-looks-design-21ffef43cb923e88e4729176294e6413.svg","alt":"Two populations, females and males, defined by the gender variable X. Each has a mean score: mu 1 for females and mu 2 for males. Independent simple random samples were taken: 150 females and 85 males.","key":"JvW1ubVTgP","urlSource":"images/gen/m16-looks-design.svg"}],"enumerator":"2","key":"cf8495xH9j"},{"type":"paragraph","position":{"start":{"line":56,"column":1},"end":{"line":56,"column":1}},"children":[{"type":"emphasis","position":{"start":{"line":56,"column":1},"end":{"line":56,"column":1}},"children":[{"type":"text","value":"Comments:","position":{"start":{"line":56,"column":1},"end":{"line":56,"column":1}},"key":"XaXXZWP8fH"}],"key":"SFPxw3IS7h"}],"key":"JzrT9aSici"},{"type":"list","ordered":true,"start":1,"spread":false,"position":{"start":{"line":58,"column":1},"end":{"line":62,"column":1}},"children":[{"type":"listItem","spread":true,"position":{"start":{"line":58,"column":1},"end":{"line":59,"column":1}},"children":[{"type":"paragraph","position":{"start":{"line":58,"column":1},"end":{"line":58,"column":1}},"children":[{"type":"text","value":"Note that this figure emphasizes how the fact that our explanatory variable is a two-valued categorical variable means that in practice we are comparing two populations (defined by these two values) with respect to our response Y.","position":{"start":{"line":58,"column":1},"end":{"line":58,"column":1}},"key":"Lc3HYJxpUp"}],"key":"giBMNuMngW"}],"key":"GPtUJPFT2g"},{"type":"listItem","spread":true,"position":{"start":{"line":60,"column":1},"end":{"line":61,"column":1}},"children":[{"type":"paragraph","position":{"start":{"line":60,"column":1},"end":{"line":60,"column":1}},"children":[{"type":"text","value":"Note that even though the problem description just says that we had 239 students, the figure tells us that there were 85 males in the sample and 150 females.","position":{"start":{"line":60,"column":1},"end":{"line":60,"column":1}},"key":"liNI40oCZZ"}],"key":"Rqq77RQqc3"}],"key":"gaofiKRfwv"},{"type":"listItem","spread":true,"position":{"start":{"line":62,"column":1},"end":{"line":62,"column":1}},"children":[{"type":"paragraph","position":{"start":{"line":62,"column":1},"end":{"line":62,"column":1}},"children":[{"type":"text","value":"Following up on comment 2, note that 85 + 150 = 235, and not 239. In these data (which are real) there are four ","position":{"start":{"line":62,"column":1},"end":{"line":62,"column":1}},"key":"fVR8JvBnEk"},{"type":"emphasis","position":{"start":{"line":62,"column":1},"end":{"line":62,"column":1}},"children":[{"type":"text","value":"missing observations","position":{"start":{"line":62,"column":1},"end":{"line":62,"column":1}},"key":"BhYTVj1Pe5"}],"key":"aZs7VABKIt"},{"type":"text","value":"—4 students for whom we do not have the value of the response variable. This could be due to a number of reasons, such as recording error or nonresponse. The bottom line is that even though data were collected from 239 students, effectively we have data from only 235.","position":{"start":{"line":62,"column":1},"end":{"line":62,"column":1}},"key":"JxBir0eiH6"}],"key":"uYAfeLNhyE"}],"key":"QnFZFb9sw9"}],"key":"KbE6rJFREa"}],"class":"tip","key":"i2VvrsEwxO"},{"type":"admonition","children":[{"type":"admonitionTitle","children":[{"type":"text","value":"Many Students Wonder: Why Not Two Separate Tests?","position":{"start":{"line":65,"column":1},"end":{"line":65,"column":1}},"key":"wnqZ0BVU46"}],"key":"SM6mAfUEEF"},{"type":"paragraph","position":{"start":{"line":68,"column":1},"end":{"line":68,"column":1}},"children":[{"type":"text","value":"Why can’t we just carry out a separate test for each of the two means and compare the results? Because the question we are asking is about the ","position":{"start":{"line":68,"column":1},"end":{"line":68,"column":1}},"key":"gy2vDovdXC"},{"type":"emphasis","position":{"start":{"line":68,"column":1},"end":{"line":68,"column":1}},"children":[{"type":"text","value":"difference","position":{"start":{"line":68,"column":1},"end":{"line":68,"column":1}},"key":"U3CQxyxnSa"}],"key":"vZcbcmOM2i"},{"type":"text","value":" between the groups, the inference must be based on a single procedure that directly compares the two means (through their difference, μ₁ − μ₂), properly accounting for the variability in both samples at once. Running two separate one-sample tests does not assess how likely the observed ","position":{"start":{"line":68,"column":1},"end":{"line":68,"column":1}},"key":"aA3hBQnJ6F"},{"type":"emphasis","position":{"start":{"line":68,"column":1},"end":{"line":68,"column":1}},"children":[{"type":"text","value":"gap","position":{"start":{"line":68,"column":1},"end":{"line":68,"column":1}},"key":"hPmnKgCW0w"}],"key":"lceOV7XhWX"},{"type":"text","value":" between the groups would be by chance.","position":{"start":{"line":68,"column":1},"end":{"line":68,"column":1}},"key":"JXFKvjELp5"}],"key":"ysY0HM1ac7"}],"class":"important","key":"DnoMKu0Ayf"},{"type":"paragraph","position":{"start":{"line":71,"column":1},"end":{"line":71,"column":1}},"children":[{"type":"text","value":"We will now introduce the two-sample t-test by going through its four steps.","position":{"start":{"line":71,"column":1},"end":{"line":71,"column":1}},"key":"nXFQ6HWsCq"}],"key":"PInoiLpyv2"}],"key":"AX1e8TyOra"}],"key":"jZN2ZGVVcY"},"references":{"cite":{"order":[],"data":{}}},"footer":{"navigation":{"prev":{"title":"Independent Samples vs. Matched Pairs","url":"/pages/m16-case-c-q-2-of-2","group":"Section 13: Inference for Relationships"},"next":{"title":"The Two-Sample t-Test: Stating the Hypotheses","url":"/pages/m16-two-indepe